window.docContents[125].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Weak-Dirichlet-Problems--The-variational-route-to-Poisson___s-equation":{"contents":"The weak theory begins with an abstract Hilbert-space equation\n\n\n\nLax–Milgram solves this equation when  is bounded and coercive.  The\nPoisson problem is obtained by taking the Hilbert space to be\n, the bilinear form to be the Dirichlet energy pairing, and\nthe load to be integration against .  Bounded-domain\nPoincaré control is the ingredient that upgrades gradient control to\ncoercivity for the full Sobolev norm.\n\nThere are then two notions of solution.  The variational equation tests\nagainst every element of ; the distributional equation tests against\ncompactly supported smooth functions and reads as .  Openness\nis needed only for the latter PDE-facing formulation.  Density of smooth\ntests proves an equivalence, allowing existence and uniqueness to be proved\nabstractly and then transported to the distributional statement.\n\nThe final results explain the energy interpretation.  Symmetry gives an exact\ncompletion of the square identity, so the weak solution is the minimizer of\nthe Dirichlet functional.  Coercivity makes that minimizer unique and also\ngives continuous dependence on the forcing datum.\n\nFor a bounded bilinear form  and a continuous dual\nload , a weak solution is an element  satisfyingThis is Evans's variational definition for a bounded bilinear form , which\nneed not come from a differential operator.\n\nBoundedness of  alone does not make the equation solvable, and the\ndefinition imposes no sign condition. The extra hypothesis is coercivity, a\nlower bound on the quadratic form ; symmetry of  is never\nrequired for existence and uniqueness, and it enters only in the energy\ninterpretation below.\n\nIf  is a real Hilbert space and, for some ,then every  has a unique weak solution\n satisfying  for all .\n\nThe load is transferred to its Riesz representative.  Mathlib's\nLax–Milgram continuous linear equivalence for the coercive form is then\ninverted, and injectivity of that equivalence gives uniqueness.\n\nWhen  is symmetric, it is itself an inner product, equivalent to the one on\n, and Lax–Milgram becomes the Riesz representation theorem in that inner\nproduct. The solution  is the minimizer of the energy\n, whose level sets are the spheres of  centered\nat . The left panel of the figure draws this in the plane, where  is a\npositive definite  matrix and the spheres are ellipses. Restricting the\nequation to a closed subspace  and solving  for\n is again a Lax–Milgram problem, because coercivity passes to\nsubspaces. Its solution  is the point where a level ellipse touches the\nline, and subtracting the two equations gives  for every\n. So  is the -orthogonal projection of , which is\ngenerally not the nearest point in the norm of . Combining this orthogonality\nwith boundedness and coercivity gives Céa's lemma,\n. The Galerkin error\nis at most a fixed multiple of the best approximation error in the subspace.\nThe right panels apply this to piecewise linear finite elements on nested\nmeshes, where the best approximation error, and with it the Galerkin error,\ndecays like the mesh size. Céa's lemma is the classical statement, checked\nnumerically in the figure.\n\n\n\nTo reach Poisson's equation the abstract space is specialized to\n and the form to the pairing of weak gradients. The form\nitself makes sense on any set; geometric restrictions on \nappear only when its lower bound is needed.\n\nOn any set , the Dirichlet form on  isThe derivatives are the canonical weak coordinate derivatives in the\ngraph-space model of .\n\nThe quadratic form  is the squared  norm of the weak\ngradient, so on its own it controls only the derivative coordinates of the\ngraph-space norm. The base  coordinate has to be recovered from the\ngradient, which is exactly what the Poincaré inequality does on a bounded\ndomain.\n\nWrite that step in the usual Sobolev norm. If\n for , then\n\n\n\nThis is coercivity: small energy forces the whole state to be small in the\nsolution norm. The zero boundary condition matters. Without it, adding an\narbitrary constant leaves the gradient energy unchanged, so that energy\ncannot control the size of the function.\n\nIf  and  is bounded, then there is\n such that\n\nThe Dirichlet form is the squared  norm of the weak gradient. On a\nbounded domain, Poincaré bounds the base  norm of  by that gradient\nnorm. Combining the two coordinates of the graph-space norm therefore yields\na positive multiple controlled by .\n\nWith coercivity established, Lax–Milgram applies to  and the load\n for each . Since that load\ndepends linearly and continuously on , the resulting assignment\n is a bounded linear operator.\n\nFor positive dimension and a bounded domain, Lax–Milgram gives a\ncontinuous linear solution operatorThe named pointwise solver is evaluation of this operator at the forcing\ndatum.\n\nThe solver is defined through the inverse of the Lax–Milgram equivalence\nrather than by the equation, so the statement that  satisfies\nthe weak equation is a theorem. Combined with the uniqueness half of\nLax–Milgram, it identifies  as the only element of  with\nthis property.\n\nIf  and  is bounded, then for every\n the named Dirichlet solver  satisfies\n\nThe solver is defined by applying the inverse Lax–Milgram equivalence to the\n load . The defining inverse identity says\nprecisely that its value satisfies the displayed weak equation for every\ntest vector.\n\nUp to this point the test functions have ranged over all of ,\nand nothing about  beyond measurability, boundedness, and positive\ndimension has been used. Reading the identity as the PDE  means\ntesting against  instead, and compactly\nsupported smooth test functions require  to be open.\n\nLet  be any set, , and\n. The distributional Poisson predicate requires\n to be open andThis is the weak test form of .\n\nThe two formulations differ only in the class of test functions. Since the\ncanonical image of  is dense in  and both\nsides of the identity are continuous in the test function, restricting to\nsmooth tests loses nothing; this equivalence needs openness of  but\nneither boundedness nor positive dimension.\n\nOn an open domain, for  and\n,\n\nOne implication evaluates the variational equation on the canonical image of\neach smooth test function.  Conversely, equality on smooth tests extends to\nall of  because that image has dense range and both sides are\ncontinuous linear functionals.\n\nExistence and uniqueness can therefore be transported from the variational\nside, where Lax–Milgram provides them, to the distributional side. The\nhypotheses are the union of those used so far: boundedness and positive\ndimension for coercivity, openness for the distributional predicate.\n\nIf  and  is open and bounded, then every\n admits exactly one  such that\n\nConstruct the named Lax–Milgram solution for the coercive Dirichlet form and\ntransport existence and uniqueness across the equivalence between the\nvariational and smooth-test formulations.\n\nExistence and uniqueness are two of the three components of well-posedness.\nThe third, continuous dependence on the data, is for a linear problem a norm\nbound on the solution operator, and coercivity supplies it, since\n and the\nright-hand side is controlled by\n.\n\nUnder the same positive-dimension and bounded-domain assumptions, there is a\nconstant , independent of , such that\n\nApply the quantitative Lax–Milgram estimate with the coercivity constant,\nthen bound the concrete  load functional by the forcing norm.\n\nBecause  is linear, , and the a priori estimate applied to\nthe difference load turns the operator bound into a Lipschitz estimate. The\nzero-forcing identity  is the degenerate case of the same linearity.\n\nOn a bounded domain of positive dimension, the Dirichlet solution\noperator sends zero forcing to zero and is Lipschitz continuous: there is a\npositive constant , depending only on the fixed domain and coercive form,\nsuch thatfor all forcing data . A parallel estimate holds in the norm of\nthe induced dual load.\n\nLinearity identifies the difference of two solutions with the solution of the\ndifference load. Apply the Lax–Milgram operator bound and then the norm bound\nfor the concrete  load functional.\n\nThe Dirichlet form is also symmetric, and symmetry allows the weak equation\nto be recast as the stationarity condition of a quadratic functional.\n\nFor any set  and , the concrete\nDirichlet functional on  isThis is the quadratic Poisson specialization of Evans's generalized\nDirichlet principle.\n\nCompleting the square in  requires , so this is\nthe first point at which symmetry of the Dirichlet form is used. Once the\nsquare is completed, the weak equation replaces  by the load and\nevery term linear in  cancels.\n\nIf  satisfies the symmetric Dirichlet weak equation, then for every\n one has the exact completion of the square identityIt contains the first-variation condition and gives the minimization\nargument directly.\n\nExpand both quadratic terms, use symmetry of the Dirichlet form, and replace\n and  by the load functional through the weak equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the identity is nonnegative, since\n. Minimality of the named\nsolution therefore follows from the identity alone; boundedness of \nenters only through the existence of , not through the inequality.\n\nOn a bounded domain of positive dimension, let \nand setThe named Lax–Milgram solution  satisfies\n for every .\n\nApply the energy identity at the named weak solution.  The remaining\nDirichlet quadratic form is a sum of squared  derivative norms and is\ntherefore nonnegative.\n\nUniqueness of the minimizer is where coercivity, and not merely nonnegativity,\nis needed. Equality  forces , and only the\nPoincaré lower bound converts vanishing Dirichlet energy into .\nConversely, every minimizer agrees with the named weak solution and therefore\nsatisfies its weak equation.\n\nOn a bounded domain of positive dimension, coercivity giveswith equality exactly when . Thus the named weak solution is the unique\nminimizer of the Dirichlet functional. More generally, minimizing  is\nequivalent to the weak Euler–Lagrange equation\n for every test direction .\n\nThe completion of the square identity reduces equality of energies to\nvanishing Dirichlet energy of the difference. Poincaré coercivity forces that\ndifference to be zero, and the weak equation identifies the minimizer in both\ndirections.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems\u0009Weak Dirichlet Problems","header":"5.4.2.3.1. The variational route to Poisson's equation","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Weak-Dirichlet-Problems--The-variational-route-to-Poisson___s-equation"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--The-divergence-theorem-on-C___-graph-domains":{"contents":"Boxes and balls are special because their boundaries have explicit\nparametrizations. A general bounded domain is handled through local graphs:\nnear each boundary point, after a linear change of coordinates, the domain is\nthe region above the graph of a  function. This is the hypothesis most\nanalysis texts place on a domain when they state the Gauss–Green theorem, and\n states the theorem for such domains in\nAppendix C.2. Every integral in the theorem is taken against intrinsic\nHausdorff surface measure, and the chart and atlas measures enter only in the\nproof.\n\n\n\nThe figure shows both halves of the statement. On the left the divergence of\nthe plotted field is identically one, so its integral is an area, and the\nflux of the same field through the outward normals reproduces that area. On\nthe right is the local picture in which the normal and the surface element\nare computed.\n\nA set  has  graph boundary if for each\n there are a linear isomorphism\n and a function\n,  near the first component of\n, such that  lies on the graph of  and, for  near\n,The isomorphism need not be orthogonal. For a vector field  on a domain\nwith a  boundary atlas, the normal flux is\n at boundary points, with  the outward\nunit normal, and zero elsewhere.\n\nExtending the flux by zero off the boundary costs nothing, because it is only\never integrated against surface measure, which lives on .\n\nLet  be bounded and open with \ngraph boundary, and write  for the -dimensional Hausdorff\nmeasure on . Let  be\ncontinuous on , with each component  on \nand with the component and its first partial derivatives uniformly\ncontinuous on . Then  is integrable on\n, the normal flux is -integrable, andThe graph hypothesis alone builds the boundary atlas that defines .\nA second form takes the atlas as given, and a third assumes  is\n on all of .\n\nFirst let  be globally  and treat one component and one direction\nat a time. Cover  by finitely many graph charts and one\nopen set compactly inside , and split the component with a smooth\npartition of unity subordinate to that cover. The interior piece has compact\nsupport in , so the integral of its derivative vanishes. On a graph\npiece, integrate first along the vertical coordinate of the chart; the\nfundamental theorem of calculus leaves the value on the graph, and the\nfactor produced by the change to graph coordinates is exactly the\nchart surface element times the corresponding component of .\nSumming the pieces gives\n, and\nsumming over  gives the vector identity.For a field that is only regular up to the boundary, uniform continuity of\nthe derivatives and the graph structure give a globally  field \nequal to  on . The divergences agree on the open set\n and the normal fluxes agree on , so the identity\nfor  is the identity for . Integrability comes from the same\nextension:  is continuous on a compact set, and the\nflux is bounded on a boundary of finite surface measure.\n\nContinuity of  on the closure is a separate hypothesis because the\nboundary side of the formula evaluates  itself on .\nUniform continuity on  gives a continuous extension to the closure,\nand the hypothesis says that  equals it.\n\nApplying the theorem to  gives Green's identities. The gradient of\n on the boundary is the continuous extension of the interior gradient,\nsince  is only assumed regular up to the boundary.\n\nLet  be as in the divergence theorem, with its boundary atlas. Let\n be  up to the boundary and continuous on , and\nlet  be  up to the boundary, with continuously extended gradient\n. Then  and\n are integrable on ,\n is -integrable, and\n\nExtend  to a globally  function and  to a globally\n field, both agreeing with the originals on the closure. Their product\nis a globally  field whose divergence on  is\n. The divergence theorem for\nglobally  fields turns its integral into the flux of\n.\n\nIf  and  are both  up to the boundary and continuous on\n, thenwith both integrands integrable.\n\nApply the first identity to  and to  and subtract. The\ngradient terms cancel by symmetry of the inner product.\n\nNeither identity asks  or  to vanish on the boundary. The\nball versions are the\ncase of a sphere, with a direct radial proof.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.14. The divergence theorem on C¹ graph domains","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--The-divergence-theorem-on-C___-graph-domains"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Problem-families-and-Euclidean-space--Problem-families-and-well-posedness":{"contents":"We use one small boundary-value problem to keep the abstract definition of\nwell-posedness concrete:\n\n\n\nTake the data space to be  with the uniform norm\n. Take the solution space to be\n with the same norm, and admit only twice continuously\ndifferentiable functions satisfying the two endpoint conditions.\nThese are choices: the equation alone does not specify them.\n\nExistence has an explicit answer. Integrating twice and choosing the\nintegration constants to enforce the endpoints gives\n\n\n\nDifferentiating this expression gives , and substitution gives\nboth endpoint values. For the constant forcing , it reduces to\n: a positive arch whose downward curvature balances the\npositive right-hand side. Uniqueness is just as concrete. The difference\nof two solutions has second derivative zero, so it is affine; an affine\nfunction vanishing at both endpoints is zero.\n\nFor continuous dependence, write the same formula as\n, where\n. Then\n\n\n\n\n\nThus the solution map is Lipschitz, hence continuous, in the specified\nuniform topologies. The figure draws the tents  whose integrals\nproduce the arch , and a difference  confined to the\nband that this arch bounds. This estimate measures changes in the values of ;\nan estimate in a norm containing derivatives would be a further statement.\nThe calculation is informal; it illustrates the three clauses of the\ndefinition. Without the endpoint\nconditions, arbitrary affine functions could be added and uniqueness would\nfail. Without the two topologies, the continuous-dependence clause would\nnot yet say what is being controlled.\n\n\n\nEquip  and  with topologies. Global Hadamard well-posedness\nfor a data-dependent admissible class  means that\nthere is a solution operatorwith three precise properties:The realization map  keeps the topology and equality of the\nsolution space separate from the raw field tested by PDEProblem. Thus \nmay be a Sobolev, Hölder, energy, quotient, or classical space. Equality in\n must already encode the intended representative convention.WellPosed is a single proposition. Its existential witness is the\ncontinuous solution operator; admissibility, the residual equations, and\nuniqueness are clauses of that proposition.\n\nEvery globally well-posed family has an admissible solution for every datum:\n\nUnpack the continuous solution operator  from the well-posedness\nhypothesis and choose .\n\nExistence uses the solution operator and its admissibility clause but not\nits continuity. Uniqueness is the remaining clause, and it applies to\narbitrary admissible solutions , not only to the value\n supplied by the operator.\n\nTwo admissible solutions for the same datum are equal:\n\nUnpack the uniqueness clause of WellPosed and apply it to  and .\n\n\n\nWe use Mathlib's Euclidean space directly:The inner product, topology, measure, and continuous-linear-map structure\nall come from Mathlib.\n\nIdentifying  with coordinate functions is an equivalence of\nunderlying types, not an isometry: the left side carries the Euclidean norm,\nwhile Mathlib equips the product type on the right with the supremum norm.\nCoordinate manipulations therefore pass through this equivalence explicitly\nrather than by definitional unfolding.\n\nThe canonical coordinate equivalence isMathlib provides it as WithLp.equiv 2 (Fin n → ℝ); the inverse\nWithLp.toLp 2 constructs a Euclidean point from its coordinate function.\n\nEvolution equations single out one coordinate for time. Rather than\nintroducing a product , we keep\nspacetime inside the same Euclidean family as , so that\nevery coordinate operator defined on Euclidean space applies to spacetime\nfields unchanged. Prepending the time coordinate to the spatial ones is done\nthrough the coordinate equivalence just described.\n\nFor  and , spacetime is assembled astimeSpaceCons t x implements this convention for the heat, wave, transport,\nand conservation-law interfaces.\n\nAssembling a point is half of the convention; the projections that undo it are\nwhat theorem statements use.\n\nThe time projection istime is an ordinary function on , so theorem statements\nuse it with no symbolic-expression layer.\n\nThe spatial projection discards coordinate zero:spatialPart is the plain function and spatialPartCLM the extensionally\nequal continuous linear map; derivative arguments use the bundled map.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators\u0009Problem families and Euclidean space","header":"3.2.1.1. Problem families and well-posedness","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Problem-families-and-Euclidean-space--Problem-families-and-well-posedness"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Fefferman___s-real-domain-alternatives":{"contents":"An admissible initial velocity on  is smooth, divergence free,\nand rapidly decreasing together with all its derivatives. An admissible force\nis smooth and rapidly decreasing in space and time together with all its\nspacetime derivatives. A datum has a global smooth finite-energy solution when\nits Navier–Stokes problem has a global classical solution whose kinetic energy\nis bounded uniformly in .Alternative (A) is the statement that for every positive viscosity and every\nadmissible initial velocity, the unforced problem has a global smooth\nfinite-energy solution. Alternative (C) is the statement that for every\npositive viscosity there are an admissible initial velocity and an admissible\nforce with no global smooth finite-energy solution. The Clay problem on\n asks for a proof of (A) or a proof of (C). Their disjunction\nalone follows classically: if (A) fails at some viscosity , the scaling\n with  maps solutions at viscosity  to\nsolutions at viscosity  and keeps zero force zero, so (C) holds at\nevery viscosity. We therefore state the two alternatives separately.The force is a smooth function on all of spacetime, and only its restriction\nto  enters the problem. A global classical solution\nsatisfies the momentum equation and incompressibility for , with\nrelative smoothness of velocity and pressure and the initial trace on\n, so no totalized ambient derivative at the initial-time boundary is\nread as a one-sided physical derivative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem\u0009The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","header":"8.7.1.4. Fefferman's real-domain alternatives","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Fefferman___s-real-domain-alternatives"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Gaussian-Heat-Kernel--The-Gaussian-Kernel":{"contents":"The Gaussian heat kernel is the canonical positive, mass-one solution used to\nconstruct heat flows on Euclidean space. Under the usual regularity and growth\nhypotheses, convolution with this kernel produces the corresponding heat\nevolution, and uniqueness theorems identify that evolution with the prescribed\nsolution. Its exponential decay drives positive-time smoothing, while\npreservation of unit mass expresses conservation of total heat.\n\n\n\nThe decreasing peak and increasing width must be read together: the total\nmass stays one as the profile spreads. Verifying the differential equation\nat positive time is a separate step from proving the mass normalization or\nthe initial limit. The\nplanar Gaussian CAS example\nhandles the first step by the same differentiation and cancellation used for\nthe polynomial and sine heat solutions. Positive time supplies its denominator\nconditions; the kernel estimates supply the integral information.\n\nConvolution with the heat kernel evolves initial data, much as convolution\nwith a fundamental solution of the Laplacian solves a stationary Poisson problem.\nEvans (2010, pp. 44–59) develops the kernel and its convolution consequences\nunder explicit analytic hypotheses.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow\u0009The Gaussian Heat Kernel","header":"6.2.2.1. The Gaussian Kernel","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Gaussian-Heat-Kernel--The-Gaussian-Kernel"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions","header":"9.2.1. Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Schoenberg___Whitney-collocation":{"contents":"The Schoenberg–Whitney inequalities place each point  strictly inside\nthe canonical support of , with the points increasing strictly. The\npositivity theorem then gives a positive diagonal, although that alone does\nnot establish nonsingularity.\n\nA family of sample points  satisfies the\nSchoenberg–Whitney condition for the degree- B-splines\n when it is strictly increasing andEach sample point then lies in the open interior of the canonical support of\nits own B-spline, so every diagonal entry  of the collocation\nmatrix is strictly positive, including at repeated interior knots.\n\nThe interlacing inequalities are sufficient for a positive determinant. They\nare not necessary under the half-open endpoint convention: a left-clamped\nB-spline can take value one at its left endpoint.\n\nIf the points  satisfy the Schoenberg–Whitney condition\nfor an arbitrary nondecreasing knot sequence, repeated knots included, thenIn particular the collocation matrix is nonsingular and the B-splines\n are linearly independent real functions.\n\nInsert  copies of each sample point so that its tracked refined\nsupport begins with  equal knots and ends strictly to their right.\nBoehm's knot-insertion formula expresses every\ncoarse B-spline as a nonnegative combination of two adjacent refined\nB-splines, so each insertion factors the coarse collocation matrix as the\nrefined collocation matrix times a bidiagonal matrix with nonnegative\nentries, and such matrices are totally nonnegative on ordered minors. At a\nfully clamped knot the refined B-splines evaluate as a Kronecker delta, so\nafter all insertions the refined collocation rows selected by the sample\npoints form an identity minor. The rectangular Cauchy–Binet formula writes\nthe coarse determinant as a sum of products of ordered minors, all\nnonnegative; tracking one strictly positive ordered minor through every\ninsertion, which the interlacing inequalities guarantee, makes the sum\nstrictly positive. Nonsingularity and the collocation criterion then give\nlinear independence.\n\nThe geometric theorem certifies collocation for B-splines at interlaced\npoints. A numerical backend often holds a concrete matrix and a computed\nfactorization instead, and wants nonsingularity without invoking any theory\nof the basis; for that purpose the factorization itself, with unit-triangular\nfactors and positive pivots, is the certificate, since its determinant is\nthe product of the pivots.\n\nFor a square matrix  over a linearly ordered commutative ring with\nstrictly ordered multiplication, a positive  certificate consists of\nmatrices ,  and pivots  withwhere  is lower triangular with unit diagonal and  is upper\ntriangular with unit diagonal. The certificate stores no determinant\nassertion; a validated backend supplies the factors, and Lean checks the\nproduct, the triangular structure, the unit diagonals, and the positivity of\nevery pivot.\n\nChecking a certificate is exact arithmetic: multiply the three factors,\ncompare with , and inspect the diagonals and the signs of the pivots. The\nfamily behind  need not consist of B-splines.\n\nA valid positive  certificate for  proveshence . When  is the collocation matrix of a finite family\nof functions at matching points over a linearly ordered field, the family is\ntherefore linearly independent.\n\nThe determinant is multiplicative, the determinant of a triangular matrix is\nthe product of its diagonal entries, which are all one, and the determinant\nof the diagonal factor is the product of the pivots, a product of strictly\npositive elements. The collocation criterion converts nonvanishing of the\ndeterminant into linear independence.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009B-Splines and Schoenberg–Whitney Collocation","header":"9.2.1.10.2. Schoenberg–Whitney collocation","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--B-Splines-and-Schoenberg___Whitney-Collocation--Schoenberg___Whitney-collocation"}});